{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Process new Bee capture",
  "description": "Fires when a sentinel file appears in .kiro/bee-inbox/ (written by the sync scripts after detecting a new or updated Bee conversation). Reads the sentinel to find the raw file path, processes per bee-processing steering, writes outputs directly to the vault, then deletes the sentinel.",
  "version": "2",
  "when": {
    "type": "fileCreated",
    "patterns": [
      ".kiro/bee-inbox/*.sentinel.md"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "A new Bee capture sentinel just appeared in .kiro/bee-inbox/. Steps:\n\n1. Read the sentinel file to get the `raw_path` and `conversation_id` from its frontmatter.\n2. Read the raw conversation file at `raw_path` using PowerShell (it lives outside the workspace in the Obsidian vault). Use `Get-Content -Raw`.\n3. Apply the bee-processing steering rules: redaction policy, three outputs (tasks, meeting notes, people notes), work-vs-personal classification.\n4. **Auto-process mode: do NOT propose before writing.** Write all three outputs directly. The sentinel's frontmatter has `auto_process: true` — honor that.\n5. For People notes, be selective: only create new notes for high-signal people (people with enough context for ~3 substantive sections). For fly-by participants with no substantive signal, skip the People note but mention them in the meeting's Things to Keep in Mind section.\n6. When all outputs are written, **delete the sentinel file** so the workspace stays clean.\n7. Summarize in one or two sentences what was created and where (e.g. '3 files written: tasks, meeting notes, updated Rahul.md'). Do not recap the meeting contents unless asked."
  }
}
